Subject: [PATCH] exynos990: keep the bootstrap TTBR0 while bringing the VM up

Mark 10 is never written and the only code between mark 41 and mark 10
is flush_mmu_tlb().  The previous version of this patch failed to apply
(0141 inserted its mark into the context) and the build kept going, so
the experiment never ran.  Retry it against the real source: skip the
switch, keep the flush, and label the marks honestly.  If the kernel
then reaches pmap_bootstrap the switch is the culprit; if mark 10 is
still missing, the flush itself (or the first store after it) is.

diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c b/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c
--- a/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/arm_vm_init.c
@@ -2308,6 +2308,6 @@
-	set_mmu_ttb(invalid_ttep & TTBR_BADDR_MASK);
-	sweet_early_mark(41, "ttbr0 switched to invalid");
+	/* bring-up: keep the bootstrap TTBR0 until the final tables are proven */
+	sweet_early_mark(41, "ttbr0 switch deferred");
 
 	flush_mmu_tlb();
-	sweet_early_mark(10, "TTBR0 off; TLB flushed");
+	sweet_early_mark(10, "TTBR0 kept; TLB flushed");
 	kva_active = TRUE;
